+# DiCello-Levitt Document Vault
+
+This repository is the starting point for the Document Vault project. It includes contribution guidance, a simple directory layout, and placeholders for the frontend and backend implementations so collaborators can start coding immediately.
+
+## Getting started
+1. Clone or pull the repository.
+2. Review [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md) for conventions and working agreements.
+3. Choose the service to work on and follow the notes in its README.
+
+## If you're new to GitHub or coding
+Use these steps to add files gradually instead of trying to paste everything at once:
+1. Decide which folder your change belongs in (for example, `frontend/` for client code or `backend/` for APIs).
+2. Create or open a single file in that folder and add your content.
+3. Save the file, then run any relevant tests (for JavaScript changes, run `npm test`).
+4. Use `git add <file>` to stage just that file, then `git commit -m "Describe your change"`.
+5. Repeat for the next file. Smaller, focused commits make it easier to review and fix issues.
+
+If you're unsure where to place a file, add a short note to the file explaining the intent and commit it. Future contributors can help reorganize as the project grows.
+
+## Repository structure
+- `frontend/` – Placeholder for client-side code.
+- `backend/` – Placeholder for server-side code and APIs.
+- `AGENTS.md` – Repository-wide guidance for contributors.
+
+## Next steps
+- Add technology-specific tooling (frameworks, linters, formatters) to each service directory.
+- Define API contracts and data models before implementing features.
+- Create nested `AGENTS.md` files if a subproject requires different rules.

